1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,295
OK, are you all settled?

2
00:00:02,295 --> 00:00:06,060
Well, first of all, welcome to Cardiff University.

3
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:09,160
I'm here to explain what we can offer you.

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:13,860
Now, as a new student at the university, you will probably need some sort

5
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:18,560
of guidance to help you to use the library effectively to study and research.

6
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:23,495
Some of you have asked about a guided tour but we find this rather

7
00:00:23,495 --> 00:00:24,200
muddles people.

8
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:30,245
So, in this first week, we run a series of talks which focus on different aspects of the library

9
00:00:30,245 --> 00:00:31,200
and its resources.

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,813
You'll also find that to get the most out of the library you really

11
00:00:35,813 --> 00:00:40,427
do need to be computer literate and so all this term we run small

12
00:00:40,427 --> 00:00:45,040
classes which will bring you up to speed on how to access the computer-loaded

13
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:45,370
information.

14
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:50,000
OK, now let me give you an outline of what's available to you.

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,360
You'll find that the computers are increasingly used as a research tool.

16
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:58,072
Many students do most of their research on the internet

17
00:00:58,072 --> 00:01:00,670
and the library computers are permanently online.

18
00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:04,803
Having found what you need, you'll find you can readily save texts on your

19
00:01:04,803 --> 00:01:07,460
personal computer space to print off when you need.

20
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:11,113
You might think that it is the fastest way to get information

21
00:01:11,113 --> 00:01:12,940
but the links can be slow.

22
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:18,333
Clearly you can find lots on there but much of it is useless information

23
00:01:18,333 --> 00:01:21,800
as it is from highly debatable sources-so be critical.

24
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:27,560
You'll also find that the library has loaded several CD-ROMS onto the computers from

25
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:30,440
specialist reference sources such as the MLA.

26
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:34,836
It means we can expand what we offer you at very little extra cost

27
00:01:34,836 --> 00:01:38,290
and saves us having to invest in more and more books.

28
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:43,157
The CD-ROMS contain exactly the same information as the reference books as the two

29
00:01:43,157 --> 00:01:44,200
are updated together.

30
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,703
Now most of you will need to refer to journal articles in your work

31
00:01:48,703 --> 00:01:53,207
and you'll find you can also access these online and we encourage you to

32
00:01:53,207 --> 00:01:53,850
do so.

33
00:01:53,850 --> 00:01:59,351
Clearly some of you will find the printed version more accessible as it sits on the shelves

34
00:01:59,351 --> 00:02:02,910
but I'm afraid the intention is to phase these out eventually.

35
00:02:02,910 --> 00:02:07,390
However, you will still be able to print off a version of the text

36
00:02:07,390 --> 00:02:09,310
rather than photocopying the journal pages.

37
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:12,360
So you must get used to working online.

38
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:17,045
Naturally we do still have the full range of classic reference books, additional to

39
00:02:17,045 --> 00:02:21,730
the CD-ROMS, for you to use and there are several copies of each one.

40
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:25,883
This is because some of you may prefer to borrow a book rather than

41
00:02:25,883 --> 00:02:27,070
sit in the library.

42
00:02:27,070 --> 00:02:31,032
There is a restricted loan time on these so that they are not missing

43
00:02:31,032 --> 00:02:32,730
from the shelves for too long.

44
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:38,097
Although there is a Section Manager for each part of the library, they are very busy

45
00:02:38,097 --> 00:02:42,794
and so, if you do get stuck looking for things, you should ask the

46
00:02:42,794 --> 00:02:43,800
relevant Cataloguing Assistant.

47
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:50,878
As your Training Supervisor, I just oversee your induction and will not be around

48
00:02:50,878 --> 00:02:52,900
after this initial week.

49
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:59,270
Some of you may be interested to know that the library is offering specialised

50
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:02,000
training sessions on writing a dissertation.

51
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,412
Obviously this is not relevant to those of you who are undergraduates;

52
00:03:06,412 --> 00:03:08,250
it is just for postgraduates.

53
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:12,035
Your department will discuss the planning stage of the dissertation-i.e.

54
00:03:12,035 --> 00:03:16,852
what you're going to do-with you and we will focus on the s tructure

55
00:03:16,852 --> 00:03:17,540
of it.

56
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:21,800
However, the training will also include some time on the computers.

57
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:24,873
I realise most of you know how to organise files

58
00:03:24,873 --> 00:03:28,560
but we can show you the different ways to run data programmes.

59
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:33,508
Your tutors will tell you at the outset how to set out the chapters they require

60
00:03:33,508 --> 00:03:38,456
but you will need to ask them how they would like you to organise the bibliography

61
00:03:38,456 --> 00:03:40,930
because it varies depending on your subject area.

62
00:03:40,930 --> 00:03:45,010
When you've got something together the trainer here will look through the draft version

63
00:03:45,010 --> 00:03:47,050
for you to see if it's OK.

64
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:52,149
And, one final point, for those of you who have registered from abroad, we

65
00:03:52,149 --> 00:03:56,520
can offer individual sessions on dissertations if you feel you need them.

66
00:03:56,520 --> 00:04:01,366
If you require language lessons then they are available from the international Centre next

67
00:04:01,366 --> 00:04:02,750
to the Law Department.
